    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "blogtextliquid",
      "custom_css": [
        "@media screen and (min-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 24px !important; margin: 0 !important; } .shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {margin-bottom: -85px; }}",
        ".shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 16px; margin-left: 0px; text-align: left;}",
        "@media screen and (max-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {min-height: 200px; padding: 16px !important; }}"
      ],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Warmth turns the volume up on everything. Heat lifts fragrance off your skin faster and carries it further, which is why a scent that felt soft and close in January can suddenly fill an entire elevator in July. None of that is the fragrance failing you, it is just how heat and skin work together. What it does mean is that a rich, heavy winter scent, all smoke and resin and cozy depth, can swing from comforting to way too much the moment the temperature climbs.</p><p>Summer is the season for the lighter stuff. Citrus, green notes, crisp aquatics, airy florals, clean musks. None of these are lightweight in the dismissive sense. They just open up in the heat instead of turning into a headache by lunch. A well-made fresh fragrance on a hot day is one of those small, reliable pleasures, right up there with the first jump into cold water.</p>"
      }
    },
